# ⛔ #756 r7: PATTERN, not another hand-maintained line. Every binary above was
# added individually, so a new host test had to remember to come here -- and
# run_756_tests was committed (mode 100644) because that step was missed. From
# a git-archive tree make then SKIPS the recipe (the file exists and looks up
# to date), ./run_756_tests gives "Permission denied", and the whole suite
# aborts. The list above is kept so the history stays readable; this is what
# makes the next one impossible.
#
# Sources are test_*.c and generators are *.py, so nothing legitimate matches
# run_* -- verified: `git ls-files | grep '(^|/)run_'` returns only binaries.
# The negations are belt-and-braces in case that convention ever changes.
run_*
!run_*.c
!run_*.h
!run_*.py
run_904_tests

# ⛔ #1201 and #1231 BOTH ADDED THIS PATTERN INDEPENDENTLY, which is the
# strongest evidence it was needed: every gen_*.h had been listed here
# individually, so each new spliced-source test had to remember to come back,
# and #1231's gen_1231_clamp_latch.h arrived UNIGNORED -- which is how one of
# them noticed. Same reason the run_* block above gives.
#
# Why it matters more for a spliced header than for a binary: a committed
# generated header still COMPILES, and make then sees a file that looks up to
# date and SKIPS the recipe that would have regenerated it. So the test goes on
# passing against code the firmware no longer contains -- precisely the failure
# the generator exists to prevent.
#
# Sources are test_*.c and generators are gen_*.py, so nothing legitimate
# matches gen_*.h -- verified: `git ls-files tests/host | grep gen_` returns
# only the .py generators. The negations are belt-and-braces in case that
# convention ever changes, exactly as above.
gen_*.h
gen_*.h.tmp
!gen_*.c
!gen_*.py
